     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div style=\"line-break:after-white-space\">\r\nToday marks 50 years since John Chambers and Rick Becker sketched the basic foundation of R. At the time, the project that became S and then R was envisioned as an interactive environment that would define a consistent interface to tools written in Fortran.\r\n In a sense, that is still what R is today but it has also grown to be so much more. Take a minute today to read the very brief recollections of the origins of everyone’s favorite environment for data computing:\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"display:block\">\r\n<div style=\"display:inline-block\" role=\"link\">\r\n<a style=\"border-radius:10px;font-family:-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;display:block;width:300px;overflow:hidden;text-decoration:none\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://blog.r-project.org/2026/05/05/s-at-50/index.html\" dir=\"ltr\" role=\"button\" width=\"300\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://blog.r-project.org/2026/05/05/s-at-50/index.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464263409000&amp;usg=AOvVaw043yN_07PAUL2u-h0S1tv2\">\r\n<table style=\"table-layout:fixed;border-collapse:collapse;width:300px;background-color:#aaacaf;font-family:-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" width=\"300\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td>\r\n<table bgcolor=\"#AAACAF\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" width=\"300\" style=\"table-layout:fixed;font-family:-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;background-color:rgba(170,172,175,1)\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:8px 0px 8px 0px\">\r\n<div style=\"max-width:100%;margin:0px 16px 0px 16px;overflow:hidden\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;font-weight:500;font-size:12px;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;text-align:left\">\r\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://blog.r-project.org/2026/05/05/s-at-50/index.html\" style=\"text-decoration:none\" target=\"_bla